Jonker told the audient void (and rec.games.roguelike.nethack):
>   I was pondering what exactly proscribes various races
>  from being certain roles. In particular, it seems to me that
>  a character of any race ought to get to be a priest, if that's
>  what they really want. As it is, you're restricted to humans
>  and elves. Seems that gnomes/dwarves and even orcs
>  ought to be able to share in the worship of a god, since they
>  all can pray, etc.
>   So maybe it's balance considerations rather than logical
>  ones. Any thoughts?

Pre-Quest (specifically, before you can max your stats), there's very
little difference between the races (orcs can eat each other, elves get
sleep resistance, that's about it).

Post-Quest, there's very little difference between each class (it's just
down to shuffling your AK around a bit, more so for monks).

So to me, limiting the available combinations of the two seems to
*increase* the variety, by providing slightly more distinctions between
each.  For one thing, it means I get to put a bit of planning into the
Tournament's multiple-ascension trophies.

